<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nuapada district</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nuapada is a district in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh">Chhattisgarh</a>, with its headquarters at the town of Nuapada. It was created in 1993 from part of Kalahandi district. Largely rural and forested, it includes Khariar, the former seat of the Khariar princely estate, and the Sunabeda wildlife sanctuary.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nuapada-district.jpg" alt="Nuapada district" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sudhansu bhusan mishra · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yogeswar_Temple,_Patora.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nuapada district lies in the west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, between latitudes 20° and 21° 5' north, and covers 3,407.5 square kilometres. It is bounded on the north, west and south by Mahasamund district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh">Chhattisgarh</a> and on the east by Bargarh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balangir">Balangir</a> and Kalahandi districts. Its plains are fringed by rugged hill ranges stretching southward that belong to the main line of the Eastern Ghats and contain plateaus about 1,200 metres high. The district has one subdivision, Nuapada, and five blocks: Khariar, Sinapali, Boden, Komna and Nuapada.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Until March 1993 the area was part of Kalahandi district, which was divided by a state notification of 27 March 1993. Its early history is that of Kalahandi. It is believed to have formed part of the ancient territory of Mahakantara, where a king named Vyaghraraja ruled when Samudra Gupta campaigned in the region in the 4th century CE. The Nala kings ruled to about 500 CE, and the Sarabhapuriya dynasty, known from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-plate records and gold coins, held the Nuapada area in the 6th century. The Somavamsis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakshina-kosala">South Kosala</a> held it from the 8th century until Rajendra Chola's invasion of 1022, after which came the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telugu-cholas">Telugu Cholas</a> and the Eastern Gangas.</p><p>Khariar had a separate story. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chauhans</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patna">Patna</a> ruled it, and in 1590 Gopal Ray founded a line of semi-independent kings there. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> took Khariar in 1741 and the British occupied it in 1818. When the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a> was formed in 1936 Khariar became the Nuapada subdivision of Sambalpur district. Kalahandi merged with Orissa on 1 January 1948, and on 1 November 1949 the Nuapada subdivision was added to a reformed Kalahandi district, from which the present district was cut in 1993.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The district headquarters is the town of Nuapada, and Khariar and Khariar Road are the other towns with notified area councils. In the 2011 census Nuapada district had a population of 610,382, of whom 33.8 per cent belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheduled-tribes">Scheduled Tribes</a> and 13.46 per cent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheduled-castes">Scheduled Castes</a>. About 58 per cent spoke Odia as a first language, 24 per cent Sambalpuri and 15 per cent Chhattisgarhi. The economy rests on agriculture, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> the main crop, supported by irrigation projects including the Upper Jonk and the Sunder dam.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nuapada district” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
